In article <2A6D7929.3048@noiro.acs.uci.edu> fredv@abba.acs.uci.edu (Fred Velijanian) writes:
>We have a 3rd party SCSI 9track tape drive (manufactured by M4) 
>connected to a VAXstation 4000/VLC. ... 
>. . .           However BACKUP causes all kinds of problems. 
>. . .           We've been talking to the vendor and they now say it's
>VMS's fault and that BACKUP will never work with SCSI 9track tape 
>drives. Can anyone tell me if this is true or not?!! DEC sells their
>own SCSI 9track drive (TS05/TS07). Does anyone know if those drive
>work fine with multivolume backups? 
>

Mostly true.  Mostly not fine.  We have a pair of 4000/200s, each with a
TSV05 9-track drive and a M7530-PA controller.  I don't know if this is 
DSSI or SCSI.  I haven't had multivolume problems, but I've had plenty of 
others.  DEC says that BACKUP is expecting more of the retry and error 
handling to be done at the controller level, as controllers get more 
sophisticated.  So it makes sense that COPY works better than BACKUP.
Our problems are that errors can
make the drive go offline, leading to mount verification and abort.
Sometimes the controller is left in a mode that will cause it to erase
the label on the next tapes mounted, BEFORE the label is read,
without operator verification.  Other cases lead to a LEF hang, in which
the BACKUP is waiting forever.

We are working with DEC on the problem.  They would prefer that we use
the TK70 for backup.  We would prefer to use 9-track, having an
abundant supply of media.  Don't try our configuration.  (Maybe the
SCSI controller makes a difference.)

