I've got someone with an old IBM DisplayWriter III (maybe II, dunno)
_without_ the serial interface card.  It has the large 5812(?) daisywheel
printer, in excellent working order. (Actually, both parts of the system
are in almost-new shape.)  The problem involved is that:
- the system is too old, and can't handle many functions that are now
  necessary for the job, ie: multi-column (mainly), graphics, etc...

She's getting a '286 PC to replace this beast with.  And, hopefully, an HP
LaserJet (II,IIP,IIIP,whatever).   However, the IBM printer puts out some
of the best quality text she (or I!) have ever seen.  Not to mention the 4
*cases* of ribbons that came with it :-)  (That's 4 cases at 12 ribbons ea.)

I'd normally just hook it up, and write a software driver for it... no
problem.  Guess what?  IBM printers that old use 20ma current loops for
interfaces!

How do I translate between either RS-232 or parallel(PC), and 20ma???
Currently, the only way I can think of is to get an olc DEC VT220 terminal
and use it's 20ma loop as a printer device, and "echo to printer" from the
serial port! :-(

Help!
