Boy, that's an understatement ("performance degradation").  I'd add the
phrase "brain damaged".

Given that the SMG$ routines aren't portable (so what else is new concerning
VMS? :-), my company wanted, nay, NEEDED, a reasonably-modern version of
curses that would operate under VMS and allow our code to also operate on
other systems.

The solution was "Aspen Curses".  Besides producing about 4x *LESS* character
I/O than the SMG$ stuff, our code now works on multiple platforms.

NOTE: the Aspen routines ARE implemented on top on SMG$ (but our code does
not know that), and a few bugs required fixing (in Aspen curses).

The info I have for Aspen Scientific (which is now defunct) is that their
code (source) is available from:

