In article <1992Jul17.225909.17520@MITL.COM>, lemke@MITL.COM (Kennedy Lemke) writes:
|> 
|> Questions:
|> 
|> 	(1) The monitors we have are DEC part number VRT19-DA.  Do
|> 	    these monitors really not work with the CX boards?  

As pointed out by others, you really should have ordered an HX board.
It's too bad you didn't find this out in advance.  I hope you can
exchange it for a CX, so not to lose money.  If you keep the CX, you'll
need a VR290 or VR295.  The VRT19 has a resolution of 1280x1024.  The
CX only has a resolution of 1024x864.  So you lose screen real estate
if you go in this direction.  The HX can be configured for the same 
resolution and refresh rate as the PX, so it's able to use the same monitor.

|> 	(2) How do I build a new kernel that understands the CX board?
|> 	    I know how to make kernels--but do I, for example, need to
|> 	    reinstall the OS with the new graphics board installed?  

Well, all you really have to do is install the new board and run doconfig
to configure your kernel.  But if you like to do things by hand, you can 
also change the line that says:

device          px0        at ibus?     vector pxintr

to:

device          fb0        at ibus?     vector fbint

I think this all you need, but I haven't done it in a while.  Again, 
doconfig does it automagically. 

You probaly already know this, but you'll also have to modify your
/etc/ttys to pick up the correct server -- Xws for 
the DEC frame buffer server.  I forget the name of the frame buffer
server on the MIT R5 tape (used to be Xcfbpmax?)
However, if you go with the HX board, you should keep in mind that 
the DEC server runs something like 4x as fast as the MIT server,
due to special code that takes advantage of the very fast graphics 
accelerator chip on it.  

regards,
-joel


