   kalisiak@acsu.buffalo.edu (Christophe M. Kalisiak) writes:

> 
> Isn't it also a VAXserver cpu? 

Yes, the VAXStation and VAXServer used the same CPU module ( not true
for 3100 series ). My "VAXStation 3200" becomes a "VAXServer 3600 Series"
when I remove the graphics adapter.

>                                 BTW, how did the prom work? Did it only
> allow certain licenses to load, or did it have hardware built-in which 
> restricted the number of users to log in (I would imagine the latter would
> be difficult with LAT and DECnet...)?
> 

  The prom just changed the SID register to a different value than for
the timesharing system. Before LMF the only place this was checked was
in special code in LOGINOUT - which had a hard coded table of maximum
users for different CPU types - where the "station" and "server" variants
had a value of 2.  N-user MicroVAX licences worked by providing a patch to
LOGINOUT.EXE to set this table to the appropriate value.

  There's been a lot of folklore over the years about how server and
station variants had the CPU crippled in some mysterious was, in fact
it's always been done entirely in software. Those servers would happily
run as many users as their timesharing cousins if you could get the proper
PAK to let them do so ( this of course is the tricky part ).

