Duff-

Regarding your question at the beginning of the 2nd paragraph in the attached
message: No, this is NOT true.  I have one of the Hitachi 660Mb drives here,
and it is possible to install ULTRIX onto it directly ... after the problems
with the DRIVE were resolved!

More details...  The Hitachi DK515C drive (670.5 Mb formatted) does not meet
the SCSI-2 recommended time for response after a device reset; the Standard
says that a device should be able to respond within 250 mS but the drive does
not respond until 500 mS after a reset.  This caused problems when trying to
boot the drive.  Although Hitachi thought that they had firmware that would
fix the problem, it was resolved by increasing the timeout period in the
system SCSI ROM code; this was done in an update for the ROM for the
DS5000/200 and should be included in the ROM for the /25.  Once this was
resolved, I was able to install directly to the Hitachi drive and then used it
as my system disk for about 4 months before moving on to another drive.

However, when installing directly to this drive, the default partitioning may
not be what you would like because there will not be an entry in either the
disktab file or the driver (see /etc/scsi_data.c) for this disk.  I believe
that if you choose the Advanced Installation option from the installation
script, you should be able to set up the drive partitioning to your liking
before actually installing the software (I haven't tried this myself).

I have worked with problems in several other 3rd-party drives besides the
DK515C.  The Hitachi DK516C (1.34 Gb) did not have the problems that I found
in the DK515 but some other problem has made it unreliable on the DS5000/200
(even seems to be system dependent).  One Seagate (CDC/Imprimis) Wren drive
would not allow a direct installation of ULTRIX because it did not respond in
the expected manner (for ULTRIX) to a mode page that did not exist (it may
have been "fixed" for some other system), while another Seagate Wren drive
(more recent) had no problems; Seagate has a "DEC variant" for the firmware of
the first drive that makes it work.  Lastly, there are some Fujitsu drives
that have a synchronous transfer mode that is enabled by a jumper (hard-
wired); although these drives work well on some systems from other
manufacturers, the SCSI-2 Standard clearly states that a device must revert to
asynchronous mode after a reset and that synchronous mode must be enabled only
after negotiation, so the drive is not Standard-compliant and will not work
with DECstations in synchronous transfer mode.

Standing on my soapbox--

Don't always assume that Digital is trying to pick the last penny from your
pocket when something does not work on SCSI.  Both of the SCSI Standards (and
the CCS document that fell in between them) have been designed by committees
of people who were dedicated to the standard while taking their own employment
seriously.  This has meant that compromises had to be reached and these
compromises often have left areas of the Standards open for interpretation; in
case you can't guess, I will tell you the results of this - 2 well-meaning
engineers in different companies can produce devices that are both
standards-compliant and incompatible!  Oops!  And then there are situations
where short-cuts were taken; one system manufacturer in the personal computer
industry (which I will not name) makes systems that are NOT compliant to
either Standard and requires SCSI devices with special firmware (problems are
usually in the area of protocol rather than wires and voltages).

Back off the soapbox--

The software engineers responsible for the SCSI interface software in
DECstations have put a considerable amount of effort into supporting 3rd-party
disks and tapes under ULTRIX (even between V4.2 and V4.2a).  They are also the
people who wrote the recently-released CAM subsystem for RISC ULTRIX, and it
is even more flexible when working with 3rd party devices than is V4.2a.  This
is a serious attempt which is limited by time and the number of devices they
have been able to try.

We will try to help the CSC to understand the reality of openness as it
relates to 3rd party SCSI devices.  That is all I can promise.

Hope this has been useful.

JimC

