in article <1992Jul13.200244.16812@socrates.umd.edu>, steves@socrates.umd.edu (Steven Sonnenberg) says:
> 
> I bought a 3100 from a netter bout 2 months ago and recently I've started to
> have some "hardware" problmes.  I have a RZ55 internal and a Fujitsu external
> drive w/ 19" B/W monitor.
> 
> The problem: Occasionally the system wont pass test #4 as follows:
> 7..6..5..4
> ??
> 
> The only solution at this point is to reboot.  The hardware manual
> states to ask a DEC representative.  If I let the machine sit for 30
> minutes, it seems to go away.
> 
> Any clues?  What is test 4?
> 
Test four is the test for maintenance contract expiration.  It checks
the date of manufacture (encoded in your system ID register), compares
it with the system date and time, and decides you are out of warranty.

Then it checks the ECO level of the CPU and memory boards, looking simply
for the number of revs in that period.  If it falls under a minimum value,
determined by the time since manufacture, you are considered out of rev.
I don't know what the expected ECO update rate is, but I expect it to be
in the range of one every six months (i.e., if your system was made two
years ago, it expects four ECOs to have been applied).

Lastly, it checks the analog value of the overtemp sensor; if it is less than
75% of nominal operating value, it decides that you just turned on the power
after a hiatus of some sort (i.e., the system is still cold).

If all these conditions are true (out of warranty, out of rev, and powered
off for any period of time), the test fails.  It is time to have DEC come
out and charge you for unnecesary upgrades and a system check out.

The reason the manual says "Ask a DEC representative" is because they didn't
want to lie to you, and they couldn't figure out how to tell the truthe
without pissing you off.  And besides, you'll have to call them anyways.

One workaround is to leave it powered on, or allow a warm up period.

sas

