> 
>>>     Could someone provide a rundown of whatever versions of the KA630
>>>   were made?  I've only ever seen printed references to the KA630-AA,
>>>   but I'm sure there was at least one other version.
> 
>   The two variants of the KA630 that I know of:
> 
> 	KA630-AA		MicroVAX 3200/3500
> 
> 	KA630-BA		VAXstation 3200/3500
> 

This is incorrect!  There is only one KA630 -- the KA630-AA which is the 
MicroVAX III CPU.  It did come in two module versions, the M7606-A and M7606-E.
Both were functionally identical -- the chip layout is the only difference.

> 
> The -BA variant was the same as the -AA variant except for the console PROM
> which constrained the system to a 2 user environment (this was, as far as I
> know, the first time DEC did this for the VAXstations). There were follow-on
> products:
> 
> 	KA640			Slower, but more functionality
> 
> 	KA650			Faster clock rate		
> 

Wrong again!

The -BA and -AA variants apply to both the KA640 and KA650 CPU's as follows:

        KA640-AA     Multi-user CPU for the MicroVAX 3300/3400

        KA640-BA     Single-user CPU for the VAXStation 3300/3400


        KA650-AA     Multi-user CPU for the MicroVAX 3500/3600

        KA650-BA     Single-user CPU for the VAXStation 3500/3600


The KA640 includes 4MB of memory, a DSSI controller and an Ethernet controller
on the board.  The KA650 doesn't have any of these features, but it is a bit
faster (2.7 MVUPS vs 2.4 for the KA640.)

Hope this clears up the confusion!

jb
