In article <1992Jul13.151424.28697@ll.mit.edu>, wjc@ll.mit.edu (Bill
Chiarchiaro) writes:

|>  Could someone provide a rundown of whatever versions of the KA630
|>were made?  I've only ever seen printed references to the KA630-AA,
|>but I'm sure there was at least one other version.

I know of five versions:

	KA630-AA	1MB onboard memory, FPU
	KA630-AB	1MB onboard memory, no FPU
	KA630-BA	256KB onboard memory, FPU
	KA630-BB	256KB onboard memory, no FPU
	KA630-SE	Re-engineered KA630-AA (lower parts count?)

So far as I know, only the -AA and -SE were actually sold.  The -B
versions were conceived as realtime boards, but by the time the KA630
came to market everyone wanted the full megabyte, although VAXELN
systems could be made to run in 256KB.

There was also the KA620-AA, identical to the KA630-AA except that
the 78032 MicroVAX II CPU chip was replaced by the 78R32 realtime
version.

