In article <1992Jul15.163444.24269@fcom.cc.utah.edu> tdownard@victoria.utah.edu (Tammy L Downard) writes:
>I am attempting to back up a VAX/VMS filesystem over the network to a tape
>drive on an RS6000.  I have been unable to find a way of accessing a remote
>tape device using BACKUP.  Does anyone know of a way to do this?  Any help
>would be appreciated.  
>
>Tammy
>
>Please respond via email.--> tdownard@icicle.utah.edu

One method would use the DECnet software which our company supplies as
part of our kiNET software [DECnet, LAT, MOP protocol support] which is
available for most UNIX platforms.

You could then from the VAX do:

  $ backup files host::[dev]tape/save/block=20480

That would work just fine.  To get the saveset back to VMS, is just a bit
harder at this point.  VMS doesn't like the filetype characteristic that
our software identifies the tape device as.  To ge the files back, you'd
have to copy the saveset from the tape drive to a file on the VMS system
and you could then restore from that.  The commands on the UNIX host would
be:

   % dap send -binary -fix=20480 /dev/tape vax::mybackup.sav

Then, on the vax you can do:

   $ backup/list mybackup.sav/save/block=20480

We have plans to support backup restore via DECnet in a future release.
The currently planned method would be:

   $ backup/list host"user pass -binary -fix=20480"::[dev]tape/save/block=20480

For more info, contact support@ki.com or call 1-410-290-0355 or 800-945-4454.
