In article <1992Jul9.210017.11172@sei.cmu.edu>, carters@ajpo.sei.cmu.edu (Scott Carter) writes...
>a) which VAXen handle H in emulation; and

All VAXes based on single chip CPU's emulate H in software. The VAX 
88xx/8700/85xx were the last VAXes to implement H in microcode. The VAX 9000
had it in microcode for a while, but I am not sure if it was removed later.

>b) have some rough idea what the emulated performance is, e.g. nominal
>VAX instructions (the ones that made the 780 "1 MIPS" :} rather than 
>about 0.5 native MIPS) to perform a single H multiply or add?

It is very slow. If my memory serves me right it was about 100x slower on the
original 780 on a per instruction basis. Entire programs depend on amount of
H content. The emulator also looks at the next instruction and does it if it is
to be emulated. This avoids the exception handling overhead. So sequences of H
instructions run faster than the same number sprinkled with non-emulated ones.

Dileep


