In article <1992Jul9.210017.11172@sei.cmu.edu>, carters@ajpo.sei.cmu.edu (Scott Carter) writes...
> [text deleted]
>Does anyone know:
> 
>a) which VAXen handle H in emulation; and
>b) have some rough idea what the emulated performance is, e.g. nominal
>VAX instructions (the ones that made the 780 "1 MIPS" :} rather than 
>about 0.5 native MIPS) to perform a single H multiply or add?

The following VAX's implemented H_floating in microcode:

	- All VAX 11/7xx's
	- All VAX 8000's
	- VAXstation 8000
	- VAX 9000

The following VAX's implemented H_floating via emulation:

	- All MicroVAX's
	- All VAXstations except VAXstation 8000
	- All VAX 4000's
	- All VAX 6000's
	- VAX 7000, 10000 (new machines, just announced)
	- All VAXft's (fault tolerant)

Emulated h_floating is AT LEAST 100X slower than hardware floating point,
and possibly worse.

Bottom line: for portability, use g_floating.

