In article <3009@ra.nrl.navy.mil>, atkinson@itd.nrl.navy.mil (Randall Atkinson) writes...
> 
>  VMS has just as many (or more) security problems than commercial
>UNIX boxes do.  I personally know of more ways into VMS 5.x than into
>SunOS.

    Just to keep this in perspective.. There may be dozens of ways into SunOS
    about which you *don't* know, so the above statement does not equate to
    the absolutism `there are more holes in VMS than in SunOS' - just that you
    *know* of more.

    I, too, know of more ways into VMS than into SunOS - but that may just be
    because I don't know doodly-squat about SunOS (having never used it).

>        Moreover, DEC doesn't appear to have much interest in closing
>the open doors.

    I'm afraid I feel impelled to take issue with this last statement.  On
    what are you basing it?

    Digital is *very* concerned about closing security holes in its software
    products.  The VMS team is particularly active about it.  If you are aware
    of a security liability and haven't reported it to Digital, it's hardly
    fair to blame them, is it?  Oh, sure - `it should never have been there in
    the first place' - but stuff happens.  Now that it's there, regrettably,
    we want to get it fixed.  Help us out..

    In case some may not be aware of it, the person to whom reports should be
    made is Rich Boren, boren@bss.enet.dec.com.  If you don't want to use
    electronic mail, I believe you can call the Colorado Telephone Support
    Centre +1-800-525-7100 and ask for him.  If you don't want to discuss the
    details over the telephone, I believe there will be no problem with
    setting up a private meeting.

    If I get the exact details, would it be of interest to anyone for me to
    post them here?  (Reply by email to coar@nephi.enet.dec.com, please.  If I
    get more than a few `yes' responses, I'll do it.)

>                 VMS used to be rated C2 by NCSC but it is no longer a
>rated system.

    This is not correct.  VMS V4.3, with the relevant security patches and in
    the evaluated configuration, is still a valid, evaluated, C2 system.  The
    NCSC doesn't evaluate an `operating system', but a specific VERSION of the
    OS under specific strictures (such as, in the case of V4.3, `no
    networking').  It's a snapshot, not a living thing.  If your environment
    is not at least as restricted as the one that the NCSC evaluated, then you
    cannot claim to be running a rated system.

    As to why no later versions have been evaluated..  This is primarily due
    to the fact that evaluations take years to complete.  (And I DO mean
    YEARS.)  The Rating Maintenance Programme (RAMP) was not functional at the
    time of the VMS V4.3 evaluation, and has only recently been close to
    functional.  Digital is working with the NCSC to get all the necessary
    ducks in a row in order to get a current version VMS evaluated, and
    then maintain the rating in subsequent releases.

