Probably not; I'd expect they are under non-dosclosure.  Given that the
systems themselves haven't been annouced yet, and the software and
compiler folks still have more work to do and time to do it in,
I expect you'll have to wait a bit longer.  Field test is always
done under non-disclosure as well.

> 
>   - Are the ISV's effectively the beta testers of the hardware/OS, or
>     can there be customers doing hardware/OS beta testing?

Formal beta testers are a different catagory than ISV's; there
are also machines in the porting centers all over the world which
people can get access to.  So ISV's are not the only people.

We also have people inside of Digital who beat up on the hardware
and software before ISV's and field test people get them (I and a number
of other people here at CRL are among these people at the moment), 
since the overhead of fixing problems is much higher once stuff 
is outside of Digital than it is inside. These people have had 
machines for quite a while now.  You could call these "alpha testers"
if you like, though we call them "wrecking crew"; our jobs are
to try to actually use these systems for real and break as much
as possible.

