Mack,
 
The information that you seek is in the DECconnect documentation 
(I would quote it, but my copy is presently in a box while we 
re-arrange my office). 
 
There is, by the way, a reason for the "funny" plug. When modular 
plugs first became popular, some people used them as an easy, 
inexpensive way to install wall outlets for data connections in 
offices. Unfortunately, RING voltage on a normal telephone pair 
(generally RED/GREEN in most one line installations) is somewhere 
between 90 and 120 VAC, well outside the acceptable limits for 
data equipment. To avoid accidents, DIGITAL proposed the "funny" 
plug as a way to make sure that people could not plug the wrong 
cord into the wrong socket. Most present day installations are 
using RJ45 data jacks, which are similarly incompatible with 
RJ11.
 
- Bob
