Message-ID: <1992Jul9.210017.11172@sei.cmu.edu>
Summary: Which VAXen use emulation rather than ucode for H?
Sender: netnews@sei.cmu.edu (Netnews)
Reply-To: carter%csvax.decnet@mdcgwy.mdc.com
Organization: McDonnell Douglas Electronic Systems Company
Date: Thu, 9 Jul 1992 21:00:17 GMT
Lines: 28

We've got a problem that can potentially be solved two ways:

Easily and cleanly using H-float

Painfully and unpleasantly using G and a lot of hackery.

Performance counts to some degree here, which is why I'm worried.  On
our 8700, a H format multiply-add pair takes on the order of ten microsecs,
which gives acceptable performance.  In some cases it is actually faster
than the hacked version using G.

However, this software must run on other machines too, including fairly
small machines (the customer benchmark machine is a 3100).  Does anyone
know:

a) which VAXen handle H in emulation; and
b) have some rough idea what the emulated performance is, e.g. nominal
VAX instructions (the ones that made the 780 "1 MIPS" :} rather than 
about 0.5 native MIPS) to perform a single H multiply or add?

Many thanks,
Scott

