The StorageServer 100 (aka NINJA) is a platform for storage management
operations based on IEEE Mass Storage Reference Model.  This product
is a system made up of off the shelf hardware components that is
driven by file migration software.  In addition to file migration
software, other storage management applications are integrated into
this solution including: DECnsr(network save and restore for on-line
client backups), Ultrix Disk Shadowing, Ultrix Disk Striping,
Prestoserve (non-volatile RAM that should at least double NFS
performance).  Soon to be announced is a suite of system management
applications that will also run on this platform.
    
The server will always be based on Digital's most cost effective
processor (MIPS and Alpha).  The optical jukebox is from HP and is
OEMed by Digital.
    
A main feature of the StorageServer 100 is that while all these
applications can be run, the file system (with HSM) is transparent to
the user and the applications.....meaning that applications including
database applications like ULTRIX SQL/Ingres can be run.  Since the
processor power is there, the system can be viewed as a compute server
rather than just a black box file server.
 
It is true that some of the file migration software is based on the
Zetaco product.  The reason for this is that during a market analysis,
the Zetaco software was far superior to the Epoch approach.  Digital
licensed Zetaco sources and has included part of these sources in it's
product.  Key differences are that Digital uses the Ultrix File System
rather than SVID.

The Digital product is based on standards.  There are no client
licenses to buy or any proprietary communication software used to
communicate with UNIX clients.  DCE will be fully supported.
Connectivity with VMS, PCs and Macs is also available, as you would
expect from Digital.
 
The Digital Storageserver implements a similar hierarchical storage
model to that of Epoch, but differs in many implementation details:"
    
The StorageServer 100 manages the optical disks like magnetic disks
rather than like WORM platters.  As a result, when you delete a file
the space on optical is automatically deallocated.  You don't have to
migrate the "good" files off the platter and then reformat the platter
to get the space back where you deleted files where resident.  (2 hour
operation for each platter?)
    
Epoch is limited to a file size equal to the greatest amount of free
space it can find on a platter.  On Magneto Optical media for
instance, the maximum file size would be 325 MB which is the side of a
platter.  StorageServer 100 boasts "Dynamic File Partitioning".  This
means that StorageServer 100 can break files up and can currently
maintains files with a maximum of 2 GB.  On Alpha, a 64 bit processor,
the 2GB limit is removed and files can be almost any size.
    
The StorageServer 100 can easily be upgraded to an Alpha based system.
The 5900 is a full height cabinet and the MIPS boards can be upgraded
to Alpha.  The DS 5000/240 is a desktop system.  If you wanted to
upgrade this system, you can slide in an Alpha based desktop processor
(the StorageServer software development for Alpha is underway now) and
you can use the 5000/240 for other applications since it is a full
RISC processor system.  I doubt if you can upgrade Epoch systems and
use the processor that readily and reuse the existing server hardware
for other computing.
    
Please take a look at Digital's StorageServer hierarchical storage
management product.  Compare it to Epoch, Zetaco and other similar
systems.  We think you will agree that Digital has built a quality
product.

-- StorageServer 100  
-- Product Development





