	Hi, kids.

	I posted a couple of queries, and the only difinitive responses
were statements to the effect that the serial ports on a DECstation only
talk 9600 baud. A 5000 will talk 19200, but a 3100 won't.

	So I did some digging, and here's what I've got:

	The unsupported slip driver that comes with ultrix 4.2 is almost
certainly a port of Van Jacobsons 'cslip' code: the internal structure 
of the files is too similar to be otherwise.

	That being the case, slattach has three undocumented flags:

	+c	Do compression

	+e	Allow compression

	+i	Do ICMP filtering

I've only tested +c, but it works fine. In my case, I just told the annex box
that I talk to to do compression, and added the flag to the slattach command
line. piece of cake.

	I'm going to stop now. If anybody else learns anything new about the
slip driver, I'd like to know. As it is, 9600 compressed slip is better then
9600 uncompressed slip, so I fell pretty good. If I only had a hack to the 
serial driver to get 19200 ...







-- 
Anybody who agrees with me deserves what they get.

