The problem was that I founded that IOZONE on VAX/VMS is very slow.

there is a general consensus that the problem comes from the
VAX C runtime library.
The author (Bill Norcott) knows the problem but he wanted a program
portable to VMS and suggest to use $QIO system calls to get maximum
performance (using asyncronous virtual file I/O and $QIO with AST).

A couple of people told me to use different option in the open or creat calls
I tried with "mbc=16" and with othere value but I got small differences.
They say that they had notable improvment.

David L. Kashtan said that:
=	This is absolutely correct. (although the VMS 5.5 version of the
=	VAX "C" runtime library may have fixed some of this) the VAX "C"
=	read() and write() functions for STREAM_LF files use RMS block
=	I/O with 512 byte buffers.  This means that all physical I/O to
=	the disk in this case is done 1 block at a time.  Thus, if you
=	can get 40 QIOs/sec out of a disk you get at best 20Kb/sec I/O
=	throughput.  You might want to try I/O to a FIXED 512-byte record
=	file (in which case RMS record I/O is used) and you will see a
=	vast improvement (you can add arguments to your file create
=	call to make the file FIXED 512-byte).  You may also be able to
=	improve the I/O performance on STREAM_LF files considerably by
=	specifying a Multi-Block count on the file open/create (again,
=	there are arguments you can add to the open or create calls
=	to do this).
I tried also this. And It works better.Now I get 200kbytes/sec wrinting
and 600 kb/sec reading using fixed size records of 8192 byte

I would like to try other program to test the I/O performance of VAX/VMS
workstation, (memory to/from CPU, memory to/from disks etc...)
..or at least some figure of test performed by you.
I don't understand why everybody speaks of MIPS, MFLOPS and SPECMARKS
and nobody is interested in I/O benchmark.

						Ciao, Michele
