My thanks to all who responded to my question regarding upgrading VMS from
CDs on LAVc nodes without local CD readers.  I wrote:

> We're interested in dropping our TK50 MDDS in favor of CDs.  Two
> nodes in our LAVc do not have local access to a CD reader (no HSC nor
> InfoServer, either).  We're concerned about VMS upgrades on these nodes.

> I believe that non-VMS distributions can be copied and VMSINSTALled from
> SYS$UPDATE.  Is the same true for VMS?  Somehow I doubt it.  Alternatively,
> can the appropriate save-sets be copied prior to the upgrade from CDs to a
> TK50, thus building a TK50 distribution?  Is a TK50 VMS distribution just
> save-sets?  Any boot information on the tape?

> So how is this done?

The preferred method is copying across the LAVc the VMS upgrade save-sets
(VMS*.%) from the CD [VMSxxx.KIT] directory to ddcu:[000000], where ddcu is
the system disk to be upgraded.  In response to the VMSINSTAL prompt as to
where the distribution is located, ddcu:[000000] is entered.

It was pointed out that not only does copying to ddcu:[000000] save time
(especially over VMSINSTALling from tape), it may well be safer than tape.
Of course, there is a price to be paid; this method requires the additional
disk space for the save-sets.

One writer noted why VMSINSTAL OPTIONS G may not be used with VMS upgrades:
> The B saveset of a VMS distribution is an image backup of a disk with just
> enough of VMS on it to boot the system, and the G option to VMSINSTAL loses
> the directory structure on which the upgrade depends.

My conjecture of creating a TK50 distribution, though slow, appears viable.
The TK50 distribution is a standalone backup kit followed by the save-sets.
The standalone backup kit is required only for first-time installations of
VMS - only the save-sets need be present for upgrades.  As a caution, one
writer noted a sensitivity to the TK50 volume labels and placement of
save-sets on the TK50 volumes by the VMS KITINSTAL procedure.

Regarding non-VMS distributions (or distributions not requiring a boot
during the installation), it was noted that the save-sets can be copied and
VMSINSTALled from most any directory.  In the case LAVc access to a CD reader,
these non-booting distributions may be VMSINSTALled directly from the cluster-
wide mounted CD reader.

